<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/4th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Coronea (394 BC)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">394 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Battle of Coronea (394 BCE) · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corinthian-war">Corinthian War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Coronea of 394 BCE was fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corinthian-war">Corinthian War</a> between a Spartan army under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agesilaus-ii">Agesilaus II</a>, newly returned from Asia, and a coalition of Thebes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> and Corinth. Agesilaus held the field after a fierce clash with the Thebans, but the victory was indecisive. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a>, who fought on the Spartan side, left an eyewitness account.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-coronea-394-bc.jpg" alt="Battle of Coronea (394 BC)" width="320" height="297" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ward · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Funeral_rites_after_the_Battle_of_Coroneia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corinthian-war">Corinthian War</a> began in 395 BCE, when Thebes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a>, Corinth and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, with Persian support and money, united against Spartan intervention in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/locris">Locris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocis">Phocis</a>. Agesilaus II was then campaigning against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a>. Recalled, he marched overland through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, and on entering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> found his way blocked on the plain near the foot of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-helicon">Mount Helicon</a> by an army of Boeotians, Athenians, Argives, Corinthians, Euboeans and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/locrians">Locrians</a>, probably about 20,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoplite">hoplites</a> against his 15,000. He had substantially more peltasts, and the cavalry of the two sides was roughly equal.</p><p>Some of his troops were troubled by an omen: some days earlier the sun had appeared crescent-shaped. Agesilaus reminded them of the recent Spartan victory at Nemea and told them the navarch Peisander had died winning a battle against the Persian fleet, though he knew Peisander had been killed in a crushing defeat at Cnidus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Agesilaus commanded the Spartans on his right, with the veterans of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ten-thousand">Ten Thousand</a> under the Spartiate Herippidas and the Asian Greeks beside them and the Orchomenians on the far left; the Argives faced the Spartans and the Thebans the Orchomenians. The veterans and the Asian Greeks routed the troops facing them, and the Argives fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-helicon">Mount Helicon</a> before the Spartans reached them. On the other wing, however, the Thebans broke the Orchomenians and reached the baggage train with its loot from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>.</p><p>Agesilaus wheeled his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> and placed it directly in their path rather than striking their flank or rear, perhaps out of his long hostility to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a>. Xenophon described how, shield pressed upon shield, they killed and were killed in turn. A few Thebans broke through to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-helicon">Mount Helicon</a>, but many others were killed on the way.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Agesilaus was wounded and carried back to his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a>. When some 80 of the enemy took refuge in a nearby temple, he ordered that they be spared. The next morning he gave out awards for valour and let a Theban delegation collect their dead, then withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocis">Phocis</a> and invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/locris">Locris</a>, where the polemarch Gylis was killed. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a>, more than 600 Boeotians and their allies fell and the Spartans lost 350 men.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Coronea (394 BC)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Corinthian War Boeotian forces killed the Spartan commander Lysander at the Battle of Haliartus in 395 BCE and fought Agesilaus II at the <b>Battle of Coronea</b> in 394 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/boeotian-league">Boeotian League · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>They stood their ground against the Spartans at the Battle of Haliartus in 395 BCE and the <b>Battle of Coronea</b> in 394 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/thebes">Thebes · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>It sided with Sparta against Thebes in the early 4th century BCE and fought at <b>Coronea</b> in 394 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/orchomenus-boeotia">Orchomenus (Boeotia)</a></li></ul></section>
